1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,180
Mandate Wallet lets an AI agent purchase an approved API service
within an owner-defined budget.

2
00:00:09,020 --> 00:00:12,800
We will connect it to Claude Desktop,

3
00:00:13,180 --> 00:00:17,400
obtain an ETH statistics sample, and produce a short brief.

4
00:00:18,180 --> 00:00:21,860
The transactions in this demonstration run on Base Sepolia.

5
00:00:22,367 --> 00:00:26,620
Prepare Claude Desktop, Rabby and Node twenty-four,

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:30,000
then start Mandate using the manual’s setup command.

7
00:00:30,820 --> 00:00:34,080
The owner needs test ETH and USDC.

8
00:00:34,380 --> 00:00:39,140
This demo uses controlled services and fixed statistics.

9
00:00:39,627 --> 00:00:43,887
Choose Testnet and Base Sepolia in the setup wizard.

10
00:00:44,427 --> 00:00:49,027
Connect the owner wallet, then review deployment, funding and
permissions.

11
00:00:49,867 --> 00:00:53,687
We transfer zero point one five test USDC to the smart account

12
00:00:53,707 --> 00:00:56,267
and fund operator gas separately.

13
00:00:56,527 --> 00:01:00,007
The task budget is zero point one five,

14
00:01:00,287 --> 00:01:03,267
each payment is capped at zero point one,

15
00:01:03,407 --> 00:01:06,647
and the session expires after two hours.

16
00:01:07,167 --> 00:01:10,267
The owner confirms these transactions in the external wallet.

17
00:01:10,840 --> 00:01:15,320
After confirmation, open the authorization transaction in BaseScan.

18
00:01:15,760 --> 00:01:19,920
Check the Base Sepolia network, transaction hash and successful
receipt.

19
00:01:20,760 --> 00:01:24,040
The contract log links the session to the approved mandate.

20
00:01:24,780 --> 00:01:28,280
The complete hash will also appear in our execution evidence.

21
00:01:28,730 --> 00:01:32,790
Download the Guided package for this Base Sepolia configuration.

22
00:01:34,010 --> 00:01:38,410
In Claude Desktop, open Settings, Extensions and Advanced settings,

23
00:01:39,130 --> 00:01:41,410
then import and enable the package.

24
00:01:42,110 --> 00:01:48,270
Its local connection is already configured, so no Agent profile or
Connection file is selected.

25
00:01:49,050 --> 00:01:51,950
Start a new conversation using this connection.

26
00:01:52,333 --> 00:01:55,497
Ask Claude to read the account context

27
00:01:55,517 --> 00:01:59,437
and quote the approved ETH sample without purchasing.

28
00:02:00,157 --> 00:02:03,737
Check the chain, account, active session and remaining budget.

29
00:02:04,297 --> 00:02:08,657
The sample costs zero point zero one test USDC.

30
00:02:09,117 --> 00:02:12,277
Installing the connector does not expand the approved authority.

31
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:14,923
Authorize the quoted purchase.

32
00:02:15,583 --> 00:02:21,063
Mandate registers the commitment before releasing a payment signature.

33
00:02:21,523 --> 00:02:24,343
The USDC transfer settles on Base Sepolia,

34
00:02:24,863 --> 00:02:28,723
but our controlled service deliberately fails its first response.

35
00:02:29,583 --> 00:02:34,303
Claude reports payment, delivery and result validation separately.

36
00:02:35,063 --> 00:02:39,043
A successful payment alone does not mean the data was delivered.

37
00:02:39,433 --> 00:02:42,747
Open the settlement transaction in BaseScan.

38
00:02:43,187 --> 00:02:46,827
Check Success, the test USDC contract,

39
00:02:47,067 --> 00:02:51,167
and the ERC-twenty transfer from the smart account to the approved
recipient

40
00:02:51,187 --> 00:02:54,827
for zero point zero one USDC.

41
00:02:55,467 --> 00:02:58,867
The transaction sender may be the facilitator paying gas;

42
00:02:59,407 --> 00:03:04,467
the token-transfer sender identifies the paying account.

43
00:03:04,867 --> 00:03:09,710
Claude resumes the same ETH step after checking its payment state.

44
00:03:10,210 --> 00:03:14,670
The original order and settlement hash are retained, and there is no
second payment.

45
00:03:15,070 --> 00:03:19,310
The service returns the sample and the result checker validates it.

46
00:03:19,690 --> 00:03:23,750
Claude writes a brief, identifies the fixed demonstration dataset,

47
00:03:24,170 --> 00:03:25,390
and cites the settlement.

48
00:03:26,007 --> 00:03:28,667
Claude prepares a revocation request.

49
00:03:29,427 --> 00:03:33,707
The owner reviews it in the wallet workspace and signs in Rabby.

50
00:03:34,607 --> 00:03:39,587
The BaseScan receipt and SessionRevoked log confirm the change.

51
00:03:40,107 --> 00:03:45,407
Future spending by this session is blocked while the completed
purchase remains recorded.

52
00:03:45,973 --> 00:03:50,173
Finally, ask Claude to attempt the unused BTC sample.

53
00:03:50,693 --> 00:03:53,253
The revoked session prevents a new payment registration.

54
00:03:54,253 --> 00:03:59,413
This refusal has no new transaction hash because it is stopped before
broadcast.

55
00:04:00,173 --> 00:04:06,373
We retain the tool result, permission state and unchanged payment
records as refusal evidence.

56
00:04:06,940 --> 00:04:13,040
The evidence package connects each recorded step to its transaction,
contract events and service outcome.

57
00:04:13,680 --> 00:04:16,600
Payment and service delivery are documented separately.

58
00:04:17,240 --> 00:04:19,380
A local practice option is also available

59
00:04:19,400 --> 00:04:22,940
for learning the workflow without public-testnet funds.
